   "text": "# The Machine That Remade the World: A History of the Printing Press\n\nFew inventions in human history have reshaped civilization as profoundly as the printing press. Before its advent, knowledge traveled at the speed of a scribe's hand\u2014slowly, expensively, and often inaccurately. After it, ideas could multiply with a speed and fidelity never before imagined. The story of the printing press is not merely a story about a machine; it is a story about how information became democratized, how religions fractured and reformed, how science accelerated, how languages standardized, and how the modern world\u2014with all its virtues and contradictions\u2014came into being. To trace the history of the printing press is to trace the history of modernity itself.\n\n## Precursors: Printing Before Gutenberg\n\nThe printing press did not emerge from a vacuum. For centuries before Johannes Gutenberg assembled his famous contraption in the mid-fifteenth century, various forms of mechanical reproduction of text and image had existed across Asia and Europe. In China, woodblock printing was already an ancient art by the time of the Song Dynasty. The earliest surviving dated printed book, the Diamond Sutra, was produced in 868 CE during the Tang Dynasty, commissioned by a man named Wang Jie for free",
