   "text": "# From Woodblock to Web: A Long History of the Printing Press\n\n## Introduction: The Machine That Remade the World\n\nFew inventions in human history have exerted as profound and far-reaching an influence as the printing press. Before its advent, knowledge in most societies was the preserve of a tiny elite\u2014scribes, monks, scholars, and court officials\u2014who copied texts by hand, a laborious process that made books scarce, expensive, and precious. The development of mechanical printing in the middle of the fifteenth century, traditionally credited to Johannes Gutenberg of Mainz, transformed this landscape utterly. Within a few decades of Gutenberg's breakthrough, presses were operating in cities across Europe, churning out books, pamphlets, indulgences, and broadsides at a speed that would have seemed miraculous to a medieval scribe. The consequences rippled outward through every domain of human activity: religion, science, politics, literature, law, and commerce were all reshaped by the new technology. The Reformation, the Scientific Revolution, the Enlightenment, and the rise of modern democratic politics are all unimaginable without the printing press. Yet the story of printing is far longer and richer than the single moment of Gutenberg's invention. It stretches back centuries before him, to the",
